WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.060
RAII is the preferred C++ model for temporary-file cleanup.

2
00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:11.560
A wrapper object can own the file handle, remember the cleanup policy, and remove the file when the owner leaves scope.

3
00:00:11.660 --> 00:00:17.340
That approach is more reliable than asking every caller to remember a manual delete.

4
00:00:17.620 --> 00:00:22.420
Scoped handles help with normal returns, early returns, and exceptions.

5
00:00:22.520 --> 00:00:30.520
If a function fails halfway through writing a temporary file, destructors can still close handles and remove partial artifacts according to the wrapper's policy.

6
00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:32.960
Ownership should be move-only when appropriate.

7
00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:37.220
A temporary file usually has one cleanup owner.

8
00:00:37.320 --> 00:00:45.080
If ownership can move from a helper to a caller, that transfer should be visible in the type and should leave exactly one object responsible for cleanup.

9
00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:48.080
Deletion timing must be explicit.

10
00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:54.180
Some designs can remove the directory entry immediately after opening while continuing to use the handle.

11
00:00:54.280 --> 00:00:57.960
Other designs need a named file so another component can read it.

12
00:00:58.060 --> 00:01:02.300
Those choices affect review, portability, and cleanup behavior.

13
00:01:02.580 --> 00:01:04.900
Cleanup should be exception-safe.

14
00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:10.200
Destructors should not throw, and cleanup failures should be handled according to project policy.

15
00:01:10.300 --> 00:01:17.500
If cleanup failure must be reported, consider an explicit close, commit, or finalize operation before destruction.

16
00:01:17.780 --> 00:01:19.700
Avoid scattered cleanup branches.

17
00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:27.240
Manual cleanup across success, error, cancellation, and shutdown paths is easy to miss during maintenance.

18
00:01:27.340 --> 00:01:33.500
A small, well-reviewed RAII wrapper makes the lifetime and deletion rule much easier to inspect.

19
00:01:33.780 --> 00:01:38.020
The wrapper should also make intentional persistence visible.

20
00:01:38.120 --> 00:01:50.520
If a temporary file becomes an output artifact, a handoff to another component, or evidence for diagnostics, that change should be represented by an explicit release or commit operation.

21
00:01:50.800 --> 00:01:52.480
Clear naming helps review.

22
00:01:52.580 --> 00:02:02.500
A type named temporary_file_owner or scoped_temp_directory communicates responsibility better than a bare path string passed through several layers of code.
